Pedagogy’s hinge in *Places of Learning: Media, Architecture, Pedagogy* is developed through built and performative thresholds that literally and figuratively put inside in relation to outside, public to private, self to other, and thereby re-work personal and social boundaries.

The cases that are returned to throughout the book as hinge work are:

* U.S. Holocaust Memorial Museum – the entrance plaza, ramp sequence and Hall of Witness / Hall of Remembrance as an architectural choreography between civic exterior and memorial interior;
* Jane Addams Hull-House Museum – the historic settlement house and its porch / front door threshold re-activated as a hinge between domestic interior, neighbourhood street and social reform public;
* Manhattan Children’s Museum’s Art Inside Out / Art Outside – a programmatic hinge in which children’s work is moved from the museum interior out onto the street fence / neighbourhood and back in;
* Anna Deavere Smith’s verbatim documentary theatre, e.g. *Fires in the Mirror* and *Twilight: Los Angeles, 1992* – a performative hinge that places performer, testimony and audience in a mobile relation to public space and history.

*Bravehearts: Men in Skirts* is discussed in the book as an embodied/community performance pedagogy, a gendered public installation used to illustrate how a temporary performative hinge reconfigures private embodiment and public display in the same way the architectural cases do.

